(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-diuretics-loop-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"The Nephron & Loop Diuretics Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which adverse effect of loop diuretics can lead to cardiac arrhythmias?","choices":["Hyponatremia","Hyperuricemia","Azotemia","Hypokalemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics increase potassium excretion, and the resulting hypokalemia can cause cardiac arrhythmias. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which mediator is stimulated by loop diuretics and contributes to their vasodilator effect?","choices":["Prostaglandin E2","Angiotensin II","Norepinephrine","Thromboxane A2"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics stimulate prostaglandin E2, which is why nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, which block prostaglandin synthesis, blunt the response. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Amiloride acts at which site?","choices":["Distal convoluted tubule","Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Potassium-sparing diuretics act in the collecting duct, where about 2 to 3 percent of the sodium is reabsorbed under aldosterone control. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where do loop diuretics interfere with the feedback signal that regulates glomerular filtration rate?","choices":["The podocytes","The glomerular basement membrane","The proximal tubule brush border","The macula densa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics block the transporter in the macula densa, altering the feedback signal that regulates filtration rate and angiotensin II. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A patient on furosemide has a high blood urea nitrogen with volume contraction. What is this adverse effect called?","choices":["Hyperuricemia","Contraction alkalosis","Hyperglycemia","Azotemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics can cause azotemia, an increase in blood urea nitrogen, partly from the contraction of blood volume. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How do loop diuretics interact with warfarin?","choices":["They compete for plasma protein binding","They induce warfarin metabolism in the liver","They block vitamin K recycling","They increase warfarin excretion in urine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics and warfarin compete for plasma protein binding, which can change the free warfarin level. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient on a loop diuretic develops hearing loss. Which structure is damaged?","choices":["The tympanic membrane","The ossicles of the middle ear","Hair cells in the cochlea","The eustachian tube"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretic ototoxicity comes from damage to the hair cells of the cochlea. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which condition is treated with a loop diuretic given together with saline?","choices":["Hypercalcemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics increase calcium excretion, so with saline they are used to treat hypercalcemia. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which electrolyte excretion do loop diuretics increase but thiazide diuretics decrease?","choices":["Potassium","Magnesium","Calcium","Sodium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics increase calcium (and magnesium) excretion, whereas thiazides decrease renal calcium excretion. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the danger of a sustained excess loss of sodium and chloride?","choices":["Volume overload and pulmonary edema","Hypertensive crisis from fluid retention","Hypernatremia with confusion","Volume depletion and cardiovascular collapse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Too little sodium and chloride in the body means too little volume, so volume depletion and cardiovascular collapse can follow. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which acute condition is relieved because a loop diuretic lowers pulmonary pressure?","choices":["Pulmonary edema","Pulmonary embolism","Asthma exacerbation","Pneumothorax"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics reduce pulmonary pressure and are used for pulmonary edema. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which mechanism contributes to hyperglycemia with loop diuretics?","choices":["Increased renal glucose reabsorption","Increased insulin secretion","Hypokalemia impairs insulin release","Reduced glucose filtration at the glomerulus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Potassium loss impairs insulin release, and reflex catecholamines and impaired peripheral glucose uptake add to the rise in blood glucose. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which diuretic class produces the largest urine output?","choices":["Thiazide diuretics","Loop diuretics","Potassium-sparing diuretics","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics are the most potent class, with urine output up to about 4 liters per day because they block the segment that reabsorbs the most sodium beyond the proximal tubule. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which skin-related adverse effects can occur with loop diuretics?","choices":["Alopecia and nail ridging","Rash and photosensitivity","Vitiligo and hyperhidrosis","Gingival overgrowth and hirsutism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Allergic reactions with a rash and photosensitivity are listed adverse effects of loop diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Furosemide acts at which site?","choices":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Thick ascending limb"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics work in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, where about 25 percent of the sodium is reabsorbed. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which new symptom should a patient on a loop diuretic report promptly?","choices":["Increased urine volume","New hearing loss","Loss of excess fluid weight","Less ankle swelling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ototoxicity from hair cell damage in the cochlea is a loop diuretic adverse effect, so hearing changes should be reported. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which drug is NOT a loop diuretic?","choices":["Ethacrynic acid","Chlorthalidone","Torsemide","Furosemide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chlorthalidone (Hygroton) is a thiazide-type diuretic; the loop diuretics are furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide and ethacrynic acid. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which diuretic continues to work when creatinine clearance falls below 30 milliliters per minute?","choices":["Acetazolamide","Furosemide","Amiloride","Spironolactone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Furosemide, like the other loop diuretics, remains effective in patients whose creatinine clearance is below 30 milliliters per minute. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which drug class blunts the natriuretic and blood pressure response to loop diuretics?","choices":["Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors","Calcium channel blockers","Proton pump inhibitors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs block the prostaglandins that contribute to the natriuretic and blood pressure response of loop diuretics. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which hormone controls the water channels of the collecting duct?","choices":["Aldosterone","Angiotensin II","Antidiuretic hormone","Atrial natriuretic peptide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antidiuretic hormone acts on its receptor to insert water channel molecules into the luminal membrane of the collecting duct. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why does a loop diuretic raise the arrhythmia risk of digoxin?","choices":["It raises potassium and calcium","It lowers potassium and magnesium","It slows digoxin absorption from the gut","It blocks digoxin binding to its pump"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loop diuretics cause hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, and low potassium and magnesium make digoxin arrhythmias more likely. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which solutes are normally reabsorbed in the proximal tubule so that they do not appear in urine?","choices":["Glucose and amino acids","Antidiuretic hormone and aldosterone","Sodium only, with no organic solutes","Weak acids and weak bases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The proximal tubule reabsorbs glucose, amino acids and organic solutes, which is why they are normally absent from urine. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
